<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  W8 (release readiness, 2026-09-12/13 optimisation plan, finding X5). This is the BASELINE
  cleartext policy, shipped in every build, debug and release alike: plain-HTTP (cleartext) traffic
  is permitted ONLY to
    - 10.0.2.2   the Android emulator's fixed alias for the host machine's own localhost
    - localhost / 127.0.0.1   the tablet talking to a server running on itself

  Android has blocked cleartext traffic by default since API 28: with no matching domain-config
  entry, every plain http:// request is rejected before it ever leaves the device
  (java.net.UnknownServiceException: CLEARTEXT communication ... not permitted).

  UNTIL THIS PASS, this same file hardcoded the real production server's bare IP address
  (72.61.107.107) in this exact spot, with NO build-type gating at all, so every build, including
  a hypothetical release build, shipped a standing cleartext exception straight to the production
  backend (tokens, the device secret, duress audio and GPS all travelling in the clear). That was
  finding X5, and it is closed here: the production IP is never named in this file, in any build
  type, again.

  A real device on the LAN talking to a real, not yet TLS, backend, the pilot Ubuntu server's own
  bare IP being the concrete case, needs a DIFFERENT, narrower, explicitly OWNER-controlled
  mechanism, not a second hardcoded entry here: see app/build.gradle.kts's allowCleartextHost val
  and its generateDebugNetworkSecurityConfig task. That mechanism:
    - reads ALLOW_CLEARTEXT_HOST from local.properties (or the environment), empty by default;
    - when set, generates a DEBUG-ONLY override of this exact resource (same package, same name)
      carrying the baseline three hosts above PLUS that one extra domain. Android resource
      resolution gives a build-type-specific source set ("debug") full priority over "main" for the
      same resource, so a release build can never pick this up no matter what;
    - AND, belt and braces, app/build.gradle.kts's own afterEvaluate guard refuses to build a
      release artifact at all while ALLOW_CLEARTEXT_HOST is non-blank, so forgetting to clear it
      before cutting a release fails loudly instead of silently being ignored.

  Once the backend is served over https:// (docs/DEPLOY_UBUNTU.md's "Adding HTTPS later" section,
  an OWNER decision on timing, not this pass's to make), ALLOW_CLEARTEXT_HOST stops being needed at
  all; this baseline file (emulator + localhost only) should stay exactly as it is even then, those
  two entries are permanent developer conveniences, not a production exception.

  Do not add more domains/IPs to this file as a shortcut. Fix TLS at the server instead.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
